#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205227 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to solve the following linear congruence:\n$4x \\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)$\n\nSince the modulus $n=5$ is fairly small, we can solve the congruence by trial and error.\n\nSubstituting the trial solutions\n\n$$\nx = 0,1,2,3,4\n$$\n\ninto the equation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\cdot 0 & ≡0≢2 & (mod5) & \\cdot \\\\ 4 \\cdot 1 & ≡4≢2 & (mod5) & \\cdot \\\\ 4 \\cdot 2 & ≡8≡5 + 3≡3≢2 & (mod5) & \\cdot \\\\ 4 \\cdot 3 & ≡12≡2 \\cdot 5 + 2≡2 & (mod5) & ✓ \\\\ 4 \\cdot 4 & ≡16≡3 \\cdot 5 + 1≡1≢2 & (mod5) & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\equiv {\\color{blue}{3}} \\:(\\text{mod}\\:5)$.\n\n**Note**: Any number congruent to $3$ modulo $5$ is a solution. For example, note that $8 \\equiv 3$, and this also satisfies our congruence:\n\n$$\n4x \\mid ≡4 \\cdot 8\\begin{vmatrix}(mod5) \\\\ ≡32\\end{vmatrix}(mod5); ≡6 \\cdot 5 + 2\\begin{vmatrix}(mod5) \\\\ ≡0 + 2\\end{vmatrix}(mod5); ≡2✓ \\mid (mod5)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a3e926f7-e704-55d9-b2e2-ba53e03f98b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9020 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205228 :tutorial/content "Now, let's consider the following linear congruence:\n\n$$\n4x \\equiv 5 \\qquad (\\text{mod}\\:21)\n$$\n\nWe can solve this congruence by multiplying both sides by the *multiplicative inverse* of $4$ modulo $21$.\n\nIn this case, we can find the multiplicative inverse by raising $4$ to positive integer exponents until we get an answer of $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4^{2} & ≡16 & (mod21) \\\\ 4^{3} & ≡4 \\cdot 16≡64≡3 \\cdot 21 + 1≡3 \\cdot 0 + 1≡1 & (mod21)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, $4 \\cdot {\\color{blue}{16}} \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:21)$, which means that $16$ is the multiplicative inverse of $4$ (modulo $21$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $16$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4x & ≡5 & (mod21) \\\\ 16 \\cdot 4x & ≡16 \\cdot 5 & (mod21) \\\\ (16 \\cdot 4)x & ≡80 & (mod21) \\\\ 1 \\cdot x & ≡3 \\cdot 21 + 17 & (mod21) \\\\ x & ≡17 & (mod21)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\equiv 17 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:21)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "3aac2865-0a7b-5435-8fbf-46867148a3ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9022 :tutorial/title "Using the Multiplicative Inverse"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205229 :tutorial/content "Not every linear congruence has a unique solution!\n\nTo determine whether a linear congruence has a single unique solution, we can use the following rule:\n\n> *A linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1$.*\n\nWe'll prove this theorem at the end of the lesson.\n\nSo, whenever we solve a linear congruence, we should first use the above rule to check that there is a unique solution. In the linear congruence $4x \\equiv 5 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:21)$, we had $a=4$ and $n=21$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(4,21)=1\n$$\n\nwhich means that our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nIn a future lesson, we'll discuss cases where linear congruences have multiple or no solutions." :tutorial/id #uuid "b6ea554b-ce27-5d20-a1c1-cbdaf42a7946" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9023 :tutorial/title "Uniqueness of Solutions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205230 :tutorial/content "Let's prove the following theorem:\n\n> *Let $a$, $b$, and $n$ integers. If $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1$, then the linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution.*\n\n**Proof**.\n\nTo prove this theorem, we must show that a solution exists and that it is unique.\n\nFirst, we prove that a solution exists:\n\n- If $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1$, then by Bézout's identity, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n$au+nv=1$.\nWe can write this result in modulo $n$ as follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}au + nv & ≡1 & (modn) \\\\ au & ≡1 & (modn)\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis means that $u$ is the multiplicative inverse of $a$ (modulo $n$).So, multiplying both sides of the original congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ by $u$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}ax & ≡b & (modn) \\\\ u \\cdot ax & ≡u \\cdot b & (modn) \\\\ (u \\cdot a)x & ≡ub & (modn) \\\\ 1 \\cdot x & ≡ub & (modn) \\\\ x & ≡ub & (modn)\\end{bmatrix}$\nTherefore, $x \\equiv ub \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n)$ is a solution.\n\nNext, we prove the solution is unique:\n\n- To prove uniqueness, we show that two solutions $x_1$ and $x_2$ to our congruence must be congruent. Suppose $x_1$ and $x_2$ are two solutions to the congruence, i.e., $ax_1 \\equiv b\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$ and $ax_2 \\equiv b\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$. We want to show that $x_1 \\equiv x_2$.Since $ax_1 \\equiv b\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$ and $ax_2 \\equiv b\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$, we have\n$ax_1 \\equiv ax_2 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$.Now, since $\\text{gcd}(a,n) = 1$, we can multiply both sides by the modular inverse of $a$ modulo $n$, which we previously denoted $u$.\n$(u \\cdot a) x_1 \\equiv (u \\cdot a) x_2 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$Since $a$ and $n$ are relatively prime, $u$ exists. Therefore, $u \\cdot a \\equiv 1\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$, and we have\n$x_1 \\equiv x_2 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,{n})$,\nas required.\n\nThus, we have shown both the existence and uniqueness of the solution to the congruence $ax \\equiv b \\pmod{n}$ when\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n) = 1\n$$\n\n." :tutorial/id #uuid "391dbc44-2826-5acc-bb2b-9dd81558d727" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9024 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Theorem"}]}
